/**

@page page_exahype_programmers_guide_coupling Coupling different solvers

\tableofcontents

A brief discussion of how to couple differerent solvers.

ExaHyPE offers the opportunity to host multiple solver files within one mesh, to run them in parallel and to couple them as they run.
This is useful for a variety of challenges:

- It pays off for applications such as UQ, where you run multiple solves for various inputs.
  It makes sense to host them in one mesh, as you pay the cost for load balancing et al only once.
  You can even make these different solvers run on different mesh levels and do a lot of the comparisons
  on-the-fly eliminating I/O bottlenecks.
- By construction, the feature is interesting if we want to couple different solvers - for 
  example in a multiphysics sense or to have different numerics for the same PDE.
  An all-time classic is the use of different numerics and PDEs in different areas of the domain
  implementing limiting.
- It can be used to extend PDEs, where different equations are coupled to each other. Rather than
  rewriting compute for a coupled PDE system, we rely on existing building blocks and wire them 
  together. These different building blocks can now even run on different meshes.

## Design decision: Coupling through auxiliary variables


<div style="background-color: #cff ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
All volumetric coupling, i.e. coupling realised by mapping cell data onto cell data, is realised through auxiliary variables.
This is a fundamental design pattern. 
</div>


If a solver A wants to exchange information with B, then A's information ends up as auxiliar data in B.
Mathematically, this can be phrased as follows: 
Assume we solve the coupled problem

@f[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q^{(A)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(B)}) + \mathcal{B}^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)}) . \nabla Q^{(A)} & = & S^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)}) \\
\frac{\partial Q^{(B)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(B)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(B)}) + \mathcal{B}^{(B)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)}) . \nabla Q^{(B)} & = & S^{(B)}(Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)})
\end{aligned}
@f]

This is ***not*** how ExaHyPE tackles this problem straightaway, unless you want to write a 
completely new code base from scratch, which basically solves one PDE over @f$ Q := ( Q^{(A)}, Q^{(B)} ) @f$.
Instead, it breaks down the problem into 

@f[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q^{(A)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(A)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(A)}}}}(Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(A)}) + \mathcal{B}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(A)}}}}(Q^{(A)}) . \nabla Q^{(A)} & = & S^{(A)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(A)}}}}(Q^{(A)}) \\
\frac{\partial Q^{(B)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(B)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(B)}}}}(Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(B)}) + \mathcal{B}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(B)}}}}(Q^{(B)}) . \nabla Q^{(B)} & = & S^{(B)}_{{\color{blue}{M^{(B)}}}}(Q^{(B)}) \\
{\color{blue}{M^{(A)}}} & = & \Pi_{B\to A}\,Q^{(B)} \\
{\color{blue}{M^{(B)}}} & = & \Pi_{A\to B}\,Q^{(A)}
\end{aligned}
@f]

or even

@f[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q^{(A)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(A)}) + \nabla . {\color{red}{\tilde{F}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(A)})}} + \mathcal{B}^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}) . \nabla Q^{(A)} + {\color{red}{\tilde{\mathcal{B}}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(A)})}} . \nabla Q^{(A)} & = & S^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}) + {\color{red}{\tilde{ S}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(A)})}} \\
\frac{\partial Q^{(B)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(B)}) + \nabla . {\color{red}{\tilde{F}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(B)})}} + \mathcal{B}^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}) . \nabla Q^{(B)} + {\color{red}{\tilde{\mathcal{B}}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(B)})}} . \nabla Q^{(B)} & = & S^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}) + {\color{red}{\tilde{ S}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(B)})}} \\
{\color{blue}{M^{(A)}}} & = & \Pi_{B\to A}\,Q^{(B)} \\
{\color{blue}{M^{(B)}}} & = & \Pi_{A\to B}\,Q^{(A)}
\end{aligned}
@f]

So here's what's happening here and what we try to express through these formulae:

1. We keep two seperate PDEs. Each single one does not depend on two Q values, but each distinguishes explicitly between the parameter that it evolves (black) and an auxiliary parameter (blue) that it does not touch.
2. We keep the PDEs without any coupling (if something like that exists) intact where possible. 
   If the PDE terms require corrections, we model them as explicit additional terms (marked by a tilde and set in red). 
   This might not always be practicable, i.e. some terms might degenerate to nop, but the intention is to reuse building blocks (term implementations) where possible.
3. The terms are coupled through auxiliary variables obtained by projecting each solver's solution
   into the other solver's auxiliary-data space, as shown in the third and fourth row.
   These projections reduce to plain copies when the corresponding function spaces coincide.
   The copy overhead can be eliminated explicitly.


Historically, we used to name auxiliary data ***material parameters***. 
We switched to auxiliary, as material suggests that data are invariant.
They are not.


As of the summer 2026, ExaHyPE allows users to attach an arbitrary number of auxiliary variables to each solver,
and indeed an auxiliary variable of one solver can be the real variable of another one.
This is both to simplify coupling, but also to avoid that users have to copy around data all the time, which only stresses the memory bandwidth.



## How coupled solvers are realised from a technical point of view

While we could add the two solvers from the equation above one by one, we typically don't do this in ExaHyPE:

<div style="background-color: #cff ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
If two solvers are coupled, we typically introduce a third Host solver which aggregates the two existing solvers
and has the responsibility to inject the additional coupling logic and terms (red).
</div>


A user adds one coupling object (a "host", e.g. exagrype.api.CCZ4WithEuler_OuterAdditiveCoupling), which then brings its embedded solvers along. 
These aggregated solvers then later will create their task graphs how to realise a feature independently,
and it is then the host who has to merge these subgraphs properly:

\dot
digraph host {
  rankdir=LR;
  node [shape=box, style=rounded, fontsize=10];
  A [label="solver A\nbuilds its own graph\n(reports it)"];
  B [label="solver B\nbuilds its own graph\n(reports it)"];
  H [label="host concatenates\nGraph.merge()"];
  C [label="host couples\ncouple(): add tasks and\ndependencies between A and B"];
  R [label="host reports the\ncoupled graph"];
  P [label="project graphs\n(compiled into mesh sweeps)"];
  A -> H; B -> H; H -> C -> R -> P;
}
\enddot

In an outer coupling, each solver has its numerical scheme of its own. The mapping of the solvers to C++ remains solver-specific.

1. Each solver builds its time stepping graph on its own and reports it. The user thus
   sees what each solver contributes.
2. The host puts the graphs together. An outer additive coupling concatenates them
   (peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.merge()), i.e. the chains of the solvers sit next to each other,
   and then adds the tasks and dependencies that tie them together in its routine _couple().
   An outer multiplicative coupling chains the graphs (one solver waits for the other one) with
   peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.append(), and has no _couple().
   Both add tasks that copy data from one solver into the auxiliary
   variables of the other one, and a final task that synchronises the time step sizes. 
4. The host reports the coupled graph, and appends it to the project's graphs.


## Running example: Euler and CCZ4

All examples below couple the same two solvers, so you can compare the arising task graphs. The solvers are

- an Euler solver (@ref exagrype.api.euler.Euler) with Finite Volumes (Rusanov flux, explicit Euler time stepping), and
- the simplest first-order CCZ4 solver (@ref exagrype.api.FOCCZ4Solver) with fourth-order Finite Differences and Runge-Kutta of second order.
  We use no additional step such as lopsided advection.

Each solver alone yields the following time stepping task graph (each box is a task, the arrows are dependencies, and the tasks
run top-down). Euler has one stage (reconstruct the halo, update, compute the eigenvalue for the next time step size, project onto the faces):

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-graph-euler.png

CCZ4 runs two Runge-Kutta stages (reconstruct, evaluate the right-hand side, prepare, project, roll over, ...) and then wraps up:

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-graph-ccz4.png

In both cases, the last task is a <i>global end-of-step</i> task which determines the next time step size. 
The coupling does not touch the numerical schemes, it only adds tasks and dependencies.
Each solver gets a set of auxiliary variables which holds the data of the other solver ("EulerFieldsForCCZ4",
"CCZ4FieldsForEuler"): this is why CCZ4 contains a task "Reconstruct auxiliary variables EulerFields stage 0", while
Euler contains "Reconstruct auxiliary variables CCZ4Fields". 
The pictures are generated by `coupling-illustrations.py` in the directory of this page.

In both outer couplings, the host adds the following building blocks:

- Two <b>prepare tasks</b> ("Prepare auxiliary variables from CCZ4/Euler"). Each copies the persistent data of one solver into the 
  auxiliary variables of the other one (with interpolation if the patch sizes differ), projects the copy onto the faces, 
  restricts it on hanging faces and rolls the faces over. This is the one place where the solvers exchange data. 
- One final task which synchronises the time step sizes: the global end-of-step task "Synchronise time step sizes" enforces the minimum of the
  time step sizes on both solvers.

The numerical scheme of each solver, i.e. the time stepping, patch size and halo, remain whatever the user picked. 
Load balancing and dynamic mesh refinement (global tasks) exist once and wrap the whole graph, but they do not show up in the pictures. 


## Outer multiplicative coupling

Multiplicative coupling means that the state of the simulation evolves according to 

@f$
  Q(t+\delta t) = Q(t) + \left( \mathcal{U}^{(A)} \circ \mathcal{U}^{(B)} \right) Q(t).
@f$

In the context of a solver, this means that solver B updates some variables as well as some auxiliary data.
Both these variables (and potentially auxiliary data) then feed into solver A (as auxiliary values) and then induce some updates.
Overall, solver B feeds into A.
One comes after the other.

We call this outer coupling as each solver above can have its own numerical scheme.
Notably, if A and B each host a higher-order integrator, these integrators are still only coupled once.
In many cases, this reduces the overall convergence order, but there are solvers which just work fine this way.


ExaHyPE internally works with the notion of a task graph, which simplies the modelling of this type of coupling dramatically.
We simply take the task graph of solver 1 and merge the one of solver 2 into it, and then wire them,
(@ref exagrype.api.CCZ4WithEuler_OuterMultiplicativeCoupling is the worked example for Euler and CCZ4;
@ref exagrype.api.FOSOSolver is the example for the inner variant, where tasks are inserted per
Runge-Kutta stage).
All the remainder (notably finding the right number of required mesh sweeps) is done by ExaHyPE and Peano under the hood.

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #cfc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  The idea that one step follows the other logically still means that calculations can run in parallel.
  ExaHyPE takes the task sequence and hands it over to an @ref peano4.api.taskgraph.compiler "task graph compiler", which then distills
  which steps can be run in parallel.
  So the sequence is logically sequential, but the compiler might find out that some steps from solver 2 can be overlapped with activities from solver 1.
</div>

<br /><br />
@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/multiplicative-coupling.png

The image above shows multiplicative coupling for two solvers: The first one advanced their
data (blue) feeding from the auxiliary variables (which might change but most of the time 
are invariant). After that, the evolved solver data become auxiliary variables to the second
solver. 

<br /><br />
<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  The same effect could have been achieved with a modified kernel for this compound.
  Howeverm then you need a compute kernel for each solver combination. It might be 
  way more elegant to keep the two solvers separate.
  
  The same pattern also could have been produced by inserting additional compute steps
  into one solver's task graph. This is the way discussed on @ref page_exahype_programmers_guide_manipulate_solver_steps.
  We typically consider them two different things: Inserting additional stes modifies
  a solver. Here we discuss the coupling of different solvers. 
</div>

### Mathematical rewrite

For an explicit Euler, it is very easy to rewrite this coupling scheme explicitly:

@f[
\begin{aligned}
M^{(A)}& := & Q^{(B)}(t) \\
Q^{(A)}(t+0.5\Delta t) & = & Q^{(A)}(t) + \Delta t \ \frac{ \partial Q^{(A)} }{\partial t}(t) \\
M^{(B)}& := & Q^{(A)}(t+0.5\Delta t) \\
Q^{(B)}(t+\Delta t) & = & Q^{(B)}(t) + \Delta t \ \frac{ \partial Q^{(B)} }{\partial t}(t) \\
Q^{(A)}(t+\Delta t) & := & Q^{(A)}(t+0.5\Delta t)
\end{aligned}
@f]

where the actual physics remains determined by 

@f[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial Q^{(A)}}{\partial t}(t) + \nabla . F^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(A)}) + \nabla . {\color{red}{\tilde{F}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(A)}, \nabla Q^{(A)})}} + \mathcal{B}^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}) . \nabla Q^{(A)} + {\color{red}{\tilde{\mathcal{B}}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(A)})}} . \nabla Q^{(A)} & = & S^{(A)}(Q^{(A)}) + {\color{red}{\tilde{ S}^{(A)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(B)}}}(Q^{(A)})}} \\
\frac{\partial Q^{(B)}}{\partial t} + \nabla . F^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(B)}) + \nabla . {\color{red}{\tilde{F}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(B)}, \nabla Q^{(B)})}} + \mathcal{B}^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}) . \nabla Q^{(B)} + {\color{red}{\tilde{\mathcal{B}}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(B)})}} . \nabla Q^{(B)} & = & S^{(B)}(Q^{(B)}) + {\color{red}{\tilde{ S}^{(B)}_{{\color{blue} M^{(A)}}}(Q^{(B)})}} \\
\end{aligned}
@f]

 
### Example: CCZ4 followed by Euler

The host @ref exagrype.api.CCZ4WithEuler_OuterMultiplicativeCoupling chains the graphs of the running example:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
prepare (from Euler) -> graph of CCZ4 -> prepare (from CCZ4) -> graph of Euler -> synchronise
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-graph-outer-multiplicative.png

Read the picture top-down:

1. The first prepare task copies the Euler solution into the auxiliary variables of CCZ4. Both initial tasks of CCZ4 wait for it.
2. CCZ4 runs both Runge-Kutta stages. It sees the Euler data of the begin of the time step. Within the two stages, it does not 
   exchange any data with Euler: the coupling is outer.
3. Once CCZ4 has projected and rolled over its new solution, the second prepare task copies the <b>updated</b> CCZ4 solution
   into the auxiliary variables of Euler. This is the multiplicative bit: Euler works with the CCZ4 state of time @f$ t+\Delta t @f$.
4. Both initial tasks of Euler wait for the second prepare task. Euler runs its single stage.
5. The synchronisation task at the end waits for the time step size computations of both solvers.

CCZ4's own end-of-step task "Compute time step size" does not sit in front of the second prepare task. 
It stays at the end of the time step, as a global end-of-step task may not have ordinary tasks depend on it. That is why the second prepare 
task waits for the last cell tasks of CCZ4 instead, and the host does not use the plain sink tasks of CCZ4's graph.
The ordering is logical: the task graph compiler is free to fuse tasks into mesh sweeps and to overlap anything which the dependencies allow.
Here, the dependencies leave little room, as everything of Euler waits for the end of CCZ4.

## Inner multiplicative coupling

Inner multiplicative coupling means that we have N different solvers and invoke them one after the other.
They are still running in sequence.
However, in this setup, the numerical scheme sits within the host.
The host decides how time advances.

Per internal step, it now calls the sequence of solvers.
So if we have Runge-Kutta of fourth order with four shots, then each shot triggers two substeps if we wire together two steps.

@todo Continue write up

## Outer additive coupling

Additive coupling means that the state of the simulation evolved according to 

@f$
  Q(t+\delta t) = Q(t) + \mathcal{U}_1 Q(t) + \mathcal{U}_2 Q(t) = (id + \mathcal{U}_1 + \mathcal{U}_2 ) Q(t).
@f$


In ExaHyPE, we do not provide a special mechanism for additive coupling. Instead, we 
model the additive coupling via data dependencies. Let A1->A2->A3->A4 be the task graph of 
solver A. B1->B2->B3 is the graph of B. We simply let the two chains run in parallel. For
this, we introduce an empty meta task C->A1|B1 and drop the arising task graph down into 
a task compiler.

If A and B work on completely different data, this is kind of trivial. If they 
share read-only data. If this is not the case, we have to copy data temporarily around.

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/additive-coupling.png


The image above illustrates the challenge: We want to evolve A and B at the same time.
A's current solution fees as auxiliary variable into B. Therefore, we first explicitly
copy A's data into a temporary field, and then we evolve the two systems in parallel.


<div style="background-color: #fcc ; padding: 10px; border: 1px solid green;">
  For an additive coupling, it is a real option to write new compute kernels that
  work on both PDEs at the same time. We then need only one set of unknowns and
  one set of auxiliary variables (even though we can keep those separate - doesn't 
  hurt). The drawback is flexibility and composability. We also miss out on the 
  parallelism intrinsical to additive schemes. 
    
  Nevertheless, it has to be flagged that such an approach migth be able to save
  on a lot of temporary data copies at startup, which makes it a viable alternative
  to consider.
</div>

The "outer" annotation to the term "additive coupling" again implies that the solvers
each own their own numerical integration scheme. 
We exchange the auxiliary variables initially, and then we let each solver march in time.
Nothing stops us from letting once advance with Finite Volumes and the other one with 
Runge-Kutta.
However, if we have to 3rd order Runge-Kutta schemes, the clue is that the solvers do not exchange
data after each intermediate step.
Instead, they only exchange data at the very start (and potentially as a final wrap-up).
They only are coupled in the outer time integration loop.


### Example: CCZ4 and Euler in parallel

The host @ref exagrype.api.CCZ4WithEuler_OuterAdditiveCoupling (driven by `benchmarks/exagrype/gauge-wave-with-Euler`) puts the graphs of the 
running example next to each other:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
prepare (from CCZ4) \             / graph of CCZ4 \
                      >-- first --<                >-- synchronise
prepare (from Euler) /             \ graph of Euler /
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

@image html documentation/exahype2/programmers-guide/coupling-graph-outer-additive.png

Read the picture top-down:

1. The two prepare tasks run in parallel. One copies CCZ4's solution into the auxiliary variables of Euler, the other one copies Euler's
   solution into the auxiliary variables of CCZ4.
2. All four initial tasks of the two solvers (the reconstructions of the solution and of the auxiliary variables of each solver) wait for 
   <b>both</b> prepare tasks. This is crucial: when a solver starts to write its persistent data, no copy reads them anymore. Hence, 
   the solvers can work on the same cells in parallel without a data race, and no temporary copy is needed.
3. The two chains (CCZ4 with two Runge-Kutta stages, Euler with one) run independently. Whoever finishes first just waits. 
   The solvers only see the state of the other one at the begin of the time step, and they do not exchange data in between their stages.
4. The synchronisation task at the end waits for both end-of-step tasks (time step size of CCZ4, CFL condition of Euler) and enforces the
   minimum of the two time step sizes on both solvers. It is a global end-of-step task as it has to run after the solvers have computed 
   their next time step size.

CCZ4 owns the global tasks (the load balancing switch and the dynamic mesh refinement), i.e. these exist once and wrap the whole graph.


### Outer multiplicative vs. outer additive in the source code

Both outer couplings are realised by the same host pattern, and in practice they differ in the wiring of the graphs only.
Everything else is identical: the host is a plain class that offers the solvers and generate_solver_realisation(), each solver receives a set of auxiliary variables that
holds the unknowns of the other solver, the numerical schemes are assigned to the solvers, and two "prepare auxiliary
variables" tasks copy, project and restrict the data onto the faces. A final global task synchronises the time step sizes.

The only difference is which tasks the prepare tasks are wired to:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# additive: both prep tasks precede the first tasks of both solvers, i.e. everything starts together
for first_task in first[A] + first[B]:
    for prep in preps:
        first_task.add_dependency(prep)

# multiplicative: a chain. B waits for A, and copies A after A has finished.
# Graph.append() makes the sources of a graph wait for all sinks of the graph before.
chain = Graph()
chain.append(prep_from_B)
chain.append(graph_of_A)
chain.append(prep_from_A)
chain.append(graph_of_B)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Consequently, an additive solver B sees the state of A at the begin of the time step, while a multiplicative B sees the updated state of A.
The additive tasks of A and B can overlap, whereas multiplicative steps are logically sequential. The final synchronisation
task depends on the end of the whole graph in both cases. Each solver's graph ends with a global end-of-step task, which
computes the time step size. In the multiplicative coupling, the prep task of B waits for the one of A, too, and the task graph compiler does not
fuse it into the mesh sweep of that task: it runs in a sweep of its own (one sweep more than if it only waited for the last cell tasks).
The additive reference is `exagrype.api.CCZ4WithEuler_OuterAdditiveCoupling`, driven by `benchmarks/exagrype/gauge-wave-with-Euler`. The AI skill
(see below) generates both flavours from the same recipe.

## Inner additive coupling

Internal additive coupling follows the pattern we have seen when we distinguish outer and inner multiplicative coupling:
Here, the time integrator sits in the host.
Users are not allowed to attach a time integrator to the aggregated solvers.

As the time integrator steps through time with potentially many Runge Kutta shots, 
each intermediate step exchanges the auxiliary data and launches the solvers' fundamental steps in parallel.


 

## Synchronise solvers

Often, solvers have to synchronise with each other. For most solvers, they might
for example want to agree on a joint time step size. For this, we have to embed 
the arising task graph into global tasks which ensure that the solver
attributes become consistent. In the outer couplings above, this is the global end-of-step task which synchronises the time step sizes 
(@ref exagrype.api.actionsets.SynchroniseTimeStepSizes). 
In inner couplings, where the host steps through the stages of a Runge-Kutta scheme, such a task can sit behind each stage.


